The year was 1903, when the Wright brothers first flew and the first Harley-Davidson motorcycle sputtered through the streets of Milwaukee.
"Mr. Suttle had one of those," one of the gray-haired men said excitedly to the other, pointing at an old photograph of the debut Harley model, a beefed up motorized bicycle known as "The Silent Grey Fellow."
The two men - J.B. Powell and Wayne Whisnant - were strolling in nostalgic wonderment Tuesday through Harley-Davidson's free, rolling museum, a semi-trailer truck filled with photos, memorabilia and seven vintage motorcycles.
Bedecked in the company's classic trademark orange, white and black, the museum hosted a parade of motorcycle lovers in the parking lot of Hampton Roads Harley-Davidson dealership at 7204 Warwick Blvd.
Tuesday's stop was one of many scheduled for the museum tour, which started in March at Daytona, Fla., said Taylor Anderson, Harley-Davidson's district manager for Virginia and Maryland.
Visitors ranged from reluctantly retired bikers, like Powell and Whisnant, to jeans-clad youngsters who roared in on modern "Hogs" to gape at the great-granddaddies of their machines.
Among them was a 1936 "Knucklehead," the model Whisnant first rode on at age 3, sitting behind his father, who ran a Harley shop in Hickory, N.C.
Whisnant's father, J.T. Whisnant, eventually became head Harley mechanic for Tidewater Motorcycles at 25th Street and Huntington Avenue in Newport News. Powell was parts man ager.
The shop sponsored the city's first organized bikers' group, the Newport News Motorcycle Club. The club was about 100 strong, was sanctioned by the American Motorcycle Association and "had the best name of any club in Virginia," said Whisnant.
Neither man still rides.
"No," said Powell when asked, uttering his only sad sound during the visit.
Whisnant, who once worked at Suttle Motor Corp. for the late Mike Suttle, the man who owned the 1903 Harley motorcycle, said he hasn't ridden in a decade.
He offered some philosophy: "As you get older, the winters get colder." Now living in North East, Md., he was in town for his mother's funeral.
The motorcycle brand he and Powell pioneered, though having endured some perilous times, is aroar with vitality.
The small Milwaukee company had gotten its big break with an Army contract during World War II, but sales dropped drastically after the war, explained Randy Bohm, parts manager of Hampton Roads Harley-Davidson.
