One of Sanford's most exclusive pieces of waterfront property has become a battleground between a developer who wants to include an assisted living facility in his building plans and residents who think it's a horrible fit for the downtown area.
Many city residents were outraged when a Lake Mary company pitched plans to build the center along Sanford's Lake Monroe waterfront. Marina Island, they argued, was one of the most scenic properties in Sanford and packed with potential at a time when the city was trying to attract visitors to its historic downtown.
A luxury hotel, they argued, would be a better draw for tourists and other visitors.
So it was no surprise that hundreds of people packed a city meeting on Aug. 11 to voice their opposition to the request by TFA IV LLC to rezone nearly seven acres on Marina Island to allow a new hotel, restaurants and shops, along with converting the old Palms Island Resort hotel into an assisted living facility.
After several hours of debate, city commissioners voted 3-to-2 to deny the request. Residents cheered afterward.
But less than a month later, city Commissioner Randy Jones is reconsidering his vote against the project.
He said a TFA representative approached him after the meeting and showed him more detailed plans, pointing out the assisted-living facility was actually a small portion of the project.
"I thought about it a lot. I lost sleep over it," Jones said. "Part of why we denied it was for this lack of information."
City commissioners then agreed to vote again on the project on Sept. 22.
"Nobody is opposed to an ALF [assisted living facility] in Sanford," said Hank Dieckhaus, a 12-year Sanford resident. But he said the site "is not the appropriate place for an ALF."
"You tell me how an ALF makes Sanford's Marina Isle a waterfront destination? I don't see it."
Marina Island is actually a peninsula that juts into the southern end of Lake Monroe near Fort Mellon Park and the downtown business district.
