A newly released Wikileaks cable reveals that in 2009, the U.S. government put pressure on Haiti not to increase its minimum wage by as much as it was planning. The story quickly went viral, as a host of websites used it as proof that America was supporting greedy capitalists, at the expense of the world��s poor. But, as always, it��s not quite that simple.
The Haitian parliament had voted to more than double the minimum wage, to 62¢ per hour. A group of factory owners — who contract for large American companies, including Hanes and Levi��s — were willing to increase wages to 31¢ per hour, but no more. The U.S. State Department got involved and
successfully lobbied
for a two-tiered minimum wage, in which textile workers would be paid $3 a day, as opposed to $5.
Although it may sound counterintuitive, for people working in a country that is still trying to recover from a devastating earthquake, massive flooding and a cholera epidemic, the State Department��s efforts to keep their wages low, was
actually a blessing
Minimum wage laws are supposed to help the poor. If every person with a job made more money, it would indeed help lift communities and entire nations out of poverty. But making labour more expensive rarely leads to companies hiring more workers. It actually shrinks the number of jobs. The next time Fruit of the Loom, or another large textile company, comes looking for a factory to produce their products, they will end up going to a country with lower wages.
When it becomes more expensive to employ workers, companies hire fewer of them. They also cut back on hours and layoff existing employees. Minimum wage laws allow
some
workers to get higher salaries, but many others will lose the job they already have, or will be unable to get one in the first place. Those who are hurt most by these laws are the young and underprivileged workers who compete for a limited supply of low-skilled jobs. Haiti��s plan to boost wages, while well-intentioned, would have resulted in some workers being somewhat less poor, but would have driven many others into outright destitution. Would this have left devastated Haiti any better off?
