The Allen High baseball program returned to the District 11 playoffs this spring for the first time in nearly two decades.
Several of the players responsible for the Canaries' resurgence are out to keep Allen in the minds of Lehigh Valley fans.
The Lehigh Valley team that heads to Philadelphia to open play in the 16-team Carpenter Cup Classic all-star showcase Wednesday features the usual mix of players from power programs like Parkland, Liberty, Whitehall and Northampton. It also includes four players from Allen, the most longtime Lehigh Valley coach Ted Plessl recalls having from the school.
Senior pitcher-infielder Justin Kratz, junior infielder Jeremy Lovera, junior outfielder Angel Ruiz and junior catcher Ivan Solis made the original cut for the Lehigh Valley team. Kratz bowed out before the tournament started Monday and was replaced by another Allen pitcher, senior Carlos Solis.
Two other Allen players, junior pitcher-infielder Erick Reyes and junior pitcher-outfielder Misael Sanchez, are alternates for Lehigh Valley. The strong representation by the Canaries comes after they had not had a player picked for the Carpenter Cup since 2001.
"We're pretty happy with what we're seeing," Plessl said Tuesday. "Obviously [Allen coach Rob] Leskosky has to be pretty happy with these players too, because I think they put Allen High School back on the map a little bit in the Lehigh Valley."
Carlos Solis is part of a pitching staff that Plessl said could probably go nine deep. Carpenter Cup rules, however, dictate that teams limit their rosters to seven pitchers.
Some of those Lehigh Valley pitchers will spend time throwing to Ivan Solis this week. He has earned raves from the staff for his work behind the plate leading up to Wednesday's game against Tri-Cape (N.J.) at the Phillies' new MLB Urban Youth Academy in FDR Park.
Leskosky will be there to watch the Solis brothers, Lovera and Ruiz try to help Lehigh Valley win two games this week to reach Monday's semifinals at Citizens Bank Park. The winner of the Lehigh Valley-Tri-Cape game will play in a quarterfinal Friday.
"For me, it's a level of respect for our kids and what our kids have done," Leskosky said. "I don't know how much past coaches pushed it, but I made sure our guys were aware of it if they were interested in doing it."
Lehigh Valley is after its fourth trip to Citizens Bank Park in the last five years. It won the Carpenter Cup in 2011, reached the semifinals in 2012 and lost in the final in 2013. Lehigh Valley dropped its opener last year to Burlington County (N.J.) 8-4 in a game shortened to seven innings by rain.
